e-incident-management walkthrough log — 2026-10-10
Worked-case lesson: no command line by design (brief §6).
The "run" is the team charter + escalation matrix + communications plan + metrics
set authored and self-reviewed in-session against NIST SP 800-61 Rev. 3's
incident-management framing (April 2025; CSF 2.0-aligned; incident response as
part of risk management).

Scope: fictional Beacon Logistics (continued from e-bcp-drp). Scenario: the
post-ransomware review found management gaps (no regulatory-clock owner, customer
notice 18h late, no scribe) — this lesson builds the missing structure.
Artifacts authored (all fictional, labeled):
  - Team charter: incident commander, operations, communications, liaisons
    (legal/HR/executive sponsor), scribe — named roles with named backups;
    per-incident role assignment; "no scribe, no SEV-1" rule.
  - Escalation matrix: SEV-1..4 with impact criteria, response targets
    (15m/30m/1h/4h), join lists, declaration authority (SOC lead up, IC down),
    lateral triggers (HR on insider suspicion; counsel from SEV-2).
  - Communications plan: war-room channel vs executive brief vs customer
    templates (3 pre-written) vs regulatory obligations checklist vs single
    public voice (COO); legal review before any customer send.
  - Metrics: MTTD, MTTR (declare-to-recover defined), escalation accuracy,
    recurrence, exercise currency — quarterly + annual review cadence.

